}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} should accept floating-point operands.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Changes since R0}

\ul{
  \li{Fixed bugs in \ref("#possible-implementation") and stress-tested it}
  \li{Referenced \ref("LWG4601") in \ref("#wording")}
  \li{Improved paper editorially}
}

\h2{Introduction}

C++ is currently an outlier among programming languages
in terms of the conversion between floating-point types and integer types.
The behavior is specified in \eelis{conv.fpint#1}:

\Bquote{
A prvalue of a floating-point type can be converted to a prvalue of an integer type.
The conversion truncates; that is, the fractional part is discarded.
The behavior is undefined\iref{ub:conv.fpint.float.not.represented}
if the truncated value cannot be represented in the destination type.
}

Notably, any infinities and NaNs result in UB,
and any value outside the representable range
(possibly as the result of some numerical instability) results in UB.

\h3{Hardware support}

The status quo actually makes sense because it allows for high performance on
a variety of architectures.
Mainstream architectures typically have an instruction for converting
floating-point numbers to integers.
Let's focus on the conversion from \tcode{float32_t} to \tcode{int32_t}.

\style{
.val-table td {
  text-align: right;
}

.center {
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}
}

\table(class="val-table center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Input}
    \th{\ref("https://www.felixcloutier.com/x86/cvttss2si"){\tt{CVTTSS2SI}}\br (x86_64)}
    \th{\ref("https://docs.riscv.org/reference/isa/v20260120/unpriv/f-st-ext.html#20-1-7-single-precision-floating-point-conversion-and-move-instructions"){\tt{FCVT.W.S}}\br (RISC-V)}
    \th{\ref("https://support.arm.com/documentation/dui0801/h/A64-Floating-point-Instructions/FCVTZS--scalar--integer-"){\tt{FCVTZS}}\br (AArch64)}
    \th{\ref("https://webassembly.github.io/spec/core/exec/numerics.html#xref-exec-numerics-op-trunc-sat-mathrm-trunc-sat-s-m-n-z"){\tt{i32.trunc_sat_s_f32}}\br (WASM)}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{small \math{\mi{x}}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{NaN}}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
  }
}

Because everything but the handling of small values which are actually representable
as the resulting integer is UB,
the \tcode{float → int} conversion can always be lowered to a single instruction.

\Bnote{
The \tcode{INT32_MIN} results on x86_64 are just an error indicator.
The IA flag is also raised, indicating an invalid instruction.

For RISC-V, the assumption is that the \tcode{RTZ} (round to zero)
rounding mode was explicitly specified.
}

\h3{Comparison with other languages}

Similar to comparing hardware capabilities,
we can also compare how other languages handle this conversion:

\table(class="val-table center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Input}
    \th{\ref("https://eel.is/c++draft/conv.fpint"){C++}}
    \th{\ref("https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#numeric-cast"){Rust}}
    \th{\ref("https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se24/html/jls-5.html#jls-5.1.3"){Java}}
    \th{\ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/language-specification/conversions#1032-explicit-numeric-conversions"){C# (checked)}}
    \th{\ref("https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/language-specification/conversions#1032-explicit-numeric-conversions"){C# (unchecked)}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MIN}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{small \math{\mi{x}}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
    \td{\tcode{trunc(\math{\mi{x}})}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\msup{\mn{2}\mn{50}}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mrow{\mo{+}\mi{∞}}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{\tcode{INT32_MAX}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\math{\mi{NaN}}}
    \td{UB}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
    \td{\tcode{0}}
    \td{exception thrown}
    \td{unspecified}
  }
}

The key insight is that the saturating behavior is considered so useful
that it is baked into the core language of Rust and Java.

\Bnote{
When specifying
\ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UsersManual.html#cmdoption-f-no-strict-float-cast-overflow"){\tt{-fno-strict-float-cast-overflow}},
Clang's behavior matches that of Rust and Java.
}

\h3{The problem}

The problem is that making almost every part of the table above undefined behavior
is not very useful for some domains,
and it is unsafe.
It is fairly common that when quantizing floating-point values to integers,
some values are outside the representable range,
especially in the domain of signal processing.
In that domain, saturating behavior would be much more useful.
Frustratingly, we have a \tcode{std::saturating_cast} function,
but that function is only defined for integer types.

\h3{Proposal}

The way to solve the problem is to extend
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} to also support floating-point types,
with the Rust and Java behavior shown above.

It would also be possible to make changes to the core language,
aligning C++ with Rust and Java,
but this would have considerable performance cost
which massive amounts of existing code would silently incur.
Users would all have to start using some new flag \tt{-fstrict-float-cast-overflow}
to restore the old behavior,
but this flag would no longer be a compliant extension because it would introduce UB
where the standard defines behavior.

\h2{Motivation}

We already see some evidence that a saturating cast from floating-point to integer is useful.
This is the behavior of the core-language conversion in Rust and Java,
and AAarch64 has an instruction which implements this behavior exactly.
In C++, one can similarly imagine many applications for this behavior,
listed below.

\h3{Quantization}

It is common in calculations related to RGB colors to represent the linearized
color values as floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1].
When stored as pixel values,
they are typically quantized to 8-bit integers.
It is also common for some values to fall outside the [0, 1] range
due to numerical instability or because the original color space had a wider gamut.
That makes the UB in the conversion from \tcode{float} to \tcode{std::uint8_t} a safety hazard.
While the problem can be solved by clamping to the range [0, 1] before the conversion,
this is pessimistic because on e.g. ARM,
the underlying \tt{FCVTZU} instruction already clamps
(saturating has a clamping effect).

\h4{Optimal output using Rust}

Looking at
\ref("https://godbolt.org/z/6a6z4h1rf"){\tt{rustc} output}
lets us compare to the theoretical optimum:
\codeblock("rust"){
pub fn clamped(x: f32) -> u8 {
    return (x.clamp(0.0, 1.0) * 255f32) as u8;
}
pub fn unclamped(x: f32) -> u8 {
    return (x * 255f32) as u8;
}
}

\codeblock("txt"){
example[ceccecd8beb92274]::clamped:
        movi    d1, #0000000000000000
        fcmp    s0, #0.0
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        mov     w9, #255
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, mi
        fmov    s1, #1.00000000
        fcmp    s0, s1
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, gt
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #255
        csel    w0, w8, w9, lo
        ret

example[ceccecd8beb92274]::unclamped:
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        mov     w9, #255
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #255
        csel    w0, w8, w9, lo
        ret
}
The output for \tcode{unclamped} shows that \code("rust"){f32 as u8}
was simply lowered to a \tt{fcvtzu} instruction,
then clamped to the range [0, 255] via \tt{cmp} and \tt{csel}.
This is the theoretical optimum we would expect.

\h4{Low-quality output using C++}

C++ developers are essentially forced to get the \tcode{clamped} output
(see \url{https://godbolt.org/z/dqofdPW7a})
because without clamping,
they would run into UB for infinities and large values:
\cppblock{
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstdint>
#include <numeric>
std::uint8_t clamped(float x) {
    return std::saturating_cast<std::uint8_t>(unsigned(std::clamp(x, 0.f, 1.f) * 255));
}
}
\codeblock("txt"){
clamped(float):
        movi    d1, #0000000000000000
        fcmp    s0, #0.0
        mov     w8, #1132396544
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, mi
        fmov    s1, #1.00000000
        fcmp    s0, s1
        fcsel   s0, s1, s0, gt
        fmov    s1, w8
        fmul    s0, s0, s1
        fcvtzu  w8, s0
        cmp     w8, #256
        csinv   w0, w8, wzr, lo
        ret
}

The frustrating part is that the \q{theoretical optimum} on ARM is known,
but there is no way to express it in the C++ language without UB.
Getting to the optimal compiler output simply through optimizations
also seems unrealistic.
If \tcode{std::saturating_cast} accepted floating-point types,
we could go straight from \tcode{float} to \tcode{std::uint8_t},
producing the same output as Rust's \code("rust"){f32 as u8} conversion.

\h3{Safety}

Another obvious problem with a UB-ridden language facility is safety.
For someone trying to avoid core language UB,
\tcode{float → int} conversions is a minefield,
and there is no clear alternative.

The proposed \tcode{std::saturating_cast} is that safer alternative:
whenever a C++ developer is unsure whether a \tcode{float → int} conversion is safe,
they can use \tcode{std::saturating_cast} to avoid UB.
For the conversions that are already well-defined,
this results in no change in behavior,
and on platforms like AAarch64 or WASM,
it results in no performance cost either.

\h3{Engine implementation}

The saturating behavior is gradually becoming the de-facto standard for how to handle
conversions from floating-point to integer types.
Languages like Rust and Java handle conversions like this and
architectures like AAarch64 and WASM have instructions for this behavior.
That makes it increasingly relevant for C++ to have such an operation as well,
to make the implementation of compilers,
WASM runtimes,
emulators,
etc. easier and more efficient.

\h2{Design}

\h3{Strategy}

The overall strategy for \tcode{std::saturating_cast}
is to match the Rust and Java behavior
shown in \ref("#comparison-with-other-languages"),
as well as the \tt{FCVTZS} and \tt{i32.trunc_sat_s_f32} instructions
shown in \ref("#hardware-support").

\h3{Handling of NaN}

The proposed behavior is to return zero for NaN inputs,
which matches the aforementioned precedent.

One could also match the RISC-V \tt{FCVT.W.S} behavior
of returning \tt{INT32_MAX} for NaN inputs instead of zero,
but there is a real benefit to keeping the result for NaN inputs distinct:
If \tcode{std::isfinite(x)} is \tcode{false} for the original input,
the result of the saturating cast also provides information about which
non-finite value was passed in
(\math{\mrow{\mo{−}\mi{∞}}}, \math{\mi{+}\mi{∞}}, or \math{\mi{NaN}}).

\h3{Floating-point exceptions and constant expressions}

The handling of floating-point exceptions should be
consistent with the rest of the standard library,
such as any functions in \header{cmath},
and should try to be consistent with the
\ref("https://support.arm.com/documentation/ddi0487/mc/-Part-C-The-AArch64-Instruction-Set/-Chapter-C7-A64-Advanced-SIMD-and-Floating-point-Instruction-Descriptions/-C7-2-Alphabetical-list-of-A64-Advanced-SIMD-and-floating-point-instructions/-C7-2-106-FCVTZS--scalar--integer-?lang=en"){\tt{FCVTZS} instruction}.
That is, if \tcode{math_errhandling} is \tcode{MATH_ERREXCEPT},
the underlying instruction should raise all the appropriate exceptions so
error handling has minimal overhead.

The Arm Architecture Reference Manual Armv8 \ref("ARM DDI 0487") specifies in
§J1.4.3.206 \tt{FPToFixed} that
\ul{
  \li{NaN, infinities, and overly large inputs result in an invalid operation exception, and}
  \li{any fractional part getting truncated results in an inexact exception.}
}
The handling of invalid operations is the same across all major architectures,
which means that if \tcode{MATH_ERREXCEPT} is active,
range checks to prevent raising exceptions may actually be necessary.

Curiously, C's Annex F leaves it unspecified whether a \tcode{float → int}
conversion raises the \q{inexact} exception,
so out of caution and to grant some implementation freedom,
we should not mandate it either.

A domain error should only take place if the input is NaN.
Otherwise, \tcode{std::saturating_cast} is quasi-useless during constant evaluation.
\tcode{static_cast} and \tcode{std::saturating_cast} would be equivalent in that case
because everything that is UB in \tcode{static_cast}
turns into a domain error in \tcode{std::saturating_cast},
and domain errors disqualify expressions from being constant expressions in the standard library.
It also doesn't make any design sense for an operation that is explicitly saturating
to report a domain error when it saturates.

\Bnote{
Regardless of the design here,
unless the user can actually observe the floating-point environment
(as communicated by \tcode{#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS}),
the implementation can simply emit \tt{FCVTZS} without worrying about raising exceptions.

On WASM,
\tcode{i32.trunc_sat_s_f32} (and nothing else) is always emitted
because there is no way to observe the floating-point environment.

On x86 and RISC-V,
there is no saturating instruction,
so runtime cost is inevitable.
}

\h3{Other saturating operations}

The rest of the saturation arithmetic library is not affected.
That is, there is no floating-point support for \tcode{std::saturating_add} etc.
These functions are simply not in scope for the paper,
and the motivation does not apply to them because floating-point operations
are already saturating
(in the sense that they clamp to \math{\mo{−}\mi{∞}} and \math{\mi{+}\mi{∞}}).

We also don't propose floating-point types as a result of \tcode{std::saturating_cast}
because converting to floating-point types is already quasi-saturating.

\h3{\tt{std::simd} overloads}

\ref("P2956R3") proposes \tcode{std::simd} overloads for saturation arithmetic,
including \tcode{std::saturating_cast}.
At the time of writing,
the paper is in LWG, so we expect it to be included in C++29.
Naturally, \tcode{std::simd::saturating_cast} should also support floating-point types.

\h3{Freestanding availability}

Unlike standard library floating-point operations,
this one should be available in freestanding implementations as well,
for a number of reasons:
\ul{
  \li{
    The reason most floating-point operations aren't freestanding is that
    they necessitate a \tcode{libm} runtime library,
    but that doesn't apply here.
    \tcode{std::saturating_cast} is either equivalent to \tcode{static_cast}
    (which is freestanding)
    in the sense that both lower to the same CPU instruction,
    or it is equivalent to a \tcode{static_cast} with some \tcode{if} statements up front.
  }
  \li{
    On some architectures,
    floating-point \tcode{std::saturating_cast} lowers to a single CPU instruction,
    so it makes no sense that it wouldn't be available \q{in kernel mode}
    when other floating-point instructions are.
  }
  \li{
    Developers should always have the ability to perform a \q{safety upgrade}
    from \tcode{static_cast} to \tcode{std::saturating_cast}
    without sacrificing portability.
  }
}

\h2{Impact on existing code}

The proposed change only relaxes the constraints on
\tcode{std::saturating_cast} to allow floating-point types.
Existing uses with integer operands are not affected.

\h2{Implementation experience}

The proposed behavior is available in LLVM as the
\ref("https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#llvm-fptoui-sat-intrinsic"){\tt{llvm.fptoui.sat.*}} and
\ref("https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#llvm-fptosi-sat-intrinsic"){\tt{llvm.fptosi.sat.*}}
family of intrinsics.
However, these are not yet exposed as a standalone Clang intrinsic.
To emit them, one must specify
\ref("https://clang.llvm.org/docs/UsersManual.html#cmdoption-f-no-strict-float-cast-overflow"){\tt{-fno-strict-float-cast-overflow}},
and that doesn't have any effect on constant evaluation,
so infinities and NaNs need explicit handling at compile time.

\h3{Possible implementation}

A pure library implementation with some \tcode{if} statements for range checks
is theoretically possible, but a waste of time from an implementer viewpoint.
The goal is to emit exactly one e.g.\tt{FCVTZS}
instruction for the conversion
(or whatever the minimal equivalent is on other architectures),
and this requires compiler intrinsics.
Nonetheless, a library implementation
(not including floating-point environment and \tcode{errno} manipulation,
as is custom for floating-point math functions)
looks as follows:

\cppblock{
\cowel_include_text("float-saturating-cast.cpp")
}

\Bnote{
This implementation has been tested for correctness
for all possible \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mn{32}}} \tcode{float} bit patterns,
converted to \tcode{int32_t} and \tcode{uint32_t}.
}

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054")
with the resolution of \ref("LWG4601") applied.

\editnote{
\ref("LWG4601") recently clarified that C++ math functions like \tcode{std::lerp}
are subject to the error handling for \header{math.h} C functions.
In general, floating-point math functions in C++ should follow those error handling conventions.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

In \eelis{version.syn}, bump the \tcode{__cpp_lib_saturation_arithmetic} feature-test macro.

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_lib_saturation_arithmetic \del{202603L} \ins{20XXXXL} // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<numeric>}}
}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[numeric]}

Change \eelis{numeric.sat.cast} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class R, class T>
  constexpr R saturating_cast(T x) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
\constraints
\tcode{R} \del{and \tcode{T} are} \ins{is a}
signed or unsigned integer type\del{s}\iref{basic.fundamental}.
\ins{\tcode{T} is a signed or unsigned integer type or
a cv-unqualified floating-point type.}

\returns
\ins{If \tcode{x} is NaN, zero.
Otherwise, let \math{\mi{t}} be the value of \tcode{x}
with the fractional part (if any) discarded.}
If \del{\tcode{x}} \ins{\math{\mi{t}}}
is representable as a value of type \tcode{R},
\del{\tcode{x}} \ins{returns \math{\mi{t}}};
otherwise, \ins{returns} either the largest or smallest representable value of type \tcode{R},
whichever is closer to
\del{the value of \tcode{x}} \ins{\math{\mi{t}}}.

\ins{\remarks
If \tcode{T} is is a floating-point type
and the implementation is hosted,
the function is subject to the treatment of error conditions
for functions in header \header{cmath}\iref{library.c}.
Floating-point exceptions may be raised
unless the value of \tcode{x} is exactly representable in type \tcode{R}.
Whether the \q{inexact} exception is raised is a property of the implementation.
A domain error occurs if \tcode{x} is NaN,
in which case a function call expression is not a constant expression.
}
}

\ins{SEE ALSO: \ref("https://cstd.eisie.net/c23#7.12.1"){ISO/IEC 9899:2024 7.12.1}}
}

\h3(show_number=false){[simd]}

If \ref("P2956R3") has been applied,
change \eelis{simd.syn} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class U, \del{\exposid{simd-integral}} \ins{\exposid{simd-vec-type}} V>
  constexpr rebind_t<U, V> saturating_cast(const V& v) noexcept;
}
}

If \ref("P2956R3") has been applied,
change \eelis{simd.alg} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
template<class U, \del{\exposid{simd-integral}} \ins{\exposid{simd-vec-type}} V>
  constexpr rebind_t<U, V> saturating_cast(const V& v) noexcept;
}

\itemdescr{
\constraints
\del{Both} \tcode{U}
\del{and \tcode{typename V::value_type} are}
\ins{is a}
signed or unsigned integer type\del{s}\iref{basic.fundamental}.
\ins{\tcode{\tcode{typename V::value_type}} is
a signed or unsigned integer type or
a cv-unqualified floating-point type.}

\returns
A \tcode{rebind_t<U, V>} where the \math{\msup{\mi{i}\mtext{th}}} element is
initialized to the result of
\tcode{saturating_cast<U>(v[\math{\mi{i}}])}
for all \math{\mi{i}} in the range [\tcode{0}, \tcode{V::size()}).
}
}

